# Week 8 — Quality Requirements and Prioritisation

## 1. Quality Requirements
This week I turned vague quality statements into measurable candidate requirements for the Community Volunteer & Event Coordination System. All targets are **proposed** and need confirmation from stakeholders.

iD | Statement | Type | Priority | Source | Rationale | Verification 
NFR01 | In a usability test with representative first-time volunteers, at least 4 out of 5 shall find an event and submit an application within 3 minutes without assistance. | Usability | Should (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | Volunteers have varied digital skills; signing up should not need help. | Task-based usability test. Depends on FR08, which is still a candidate. 
NFR02 | With 25 concurrent users, at least 95% of event searches by date and town shall display results within 3 seconds. | Performance | Should (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | Slow search discourages volunteers from browsing events. | Timed test on a sample event list; record the time between the search request and the displayed results; at least 95 of 100 attempts within 3 s. 
NFR03 | Volunteer phone numbers and email addresses shall be visible only to authorised users who need them for their assigned role. | Privacy / Security | Must (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | Volunteer contact details are personal data; the Privacy Officer concern was raised in Week 1. | Access-control test with different accounts volunteer, organiser, unauthorised user. Who counts as authorised is an open question. 
NFR04 | All controls in the application journey (browse, select role, submit) shall be operable using the keyboard only. | Accessibility | Should (provisional) | [PROPOSED — confirm with stakeholders] | Some volunteers cannot use a mouse. | Complete the journey with the keyboard only; focus stays visible. This is not a full WCAG 2.2 assessment. 

The numbers in NFR01 and NFR02 are examples, not agreed targets. Access-control wording in NFR03 could also be treated as a functional requirement with a security rationale; I classify it as Privacy / Security because it constrains who can see the data.

## 2. MoSCoW Prioritisation
These priorities are provisional and need stakeholder confirmation.

Priority | Items | Reason / workaround 

Must | FR01 Account Registration | Without accounts there are no volunteers in the system. 
Must | FR02 Volunteer Profile Management | Skills and availability are needed to match volunteers to roles. 
Must | FR04 Event and Role Management | Without events and roles there is nothing to apply for. 
Must (provisional) | FR05 Qualification Verification | Core safety rule for restricted roles. It depends on the open question of who verifies qualifications. 
Must (provisional) | FR08 Submit Application | This is the volunteer's main task, but it is still a candidate pending confirmation. 
Should | FR03 Upload Qualification Evidence | Important, but evidence could be recorded by an authorised person instead. This is also a dependency of FR05. 
Should | FR06 Event Change Notifications | Valuable; organisers could contact volunteers directly as a workaround. 
Should | FR07 Roster Management | Useful for organisers; a spreadsheet could be used temporarily. 
Could | Waitlist, calendar view (candidates, no IDs yet) | Not supported by the scenario; decisions needed. 
Won't (this release) | None assigned |No release exclusions have been confirmed. 


## 3. Prototype Updates

The prototype is used to explore requirements not to show how they will be implemented

## 4. Open Questions

Question | Who can answer 

1 | What search response time and load do stakeholders expect? | Event Organiser, IT Support 
2 | How should usability be measured, and who are the representative volunteers? | Volunteers, Event Organiser 
3 | Who verifies volunteer qualifications? | Event Organiser, Privacy Officer 
4 | What should happen when Garda vetting status is pending or expired? | Event Organiser, Privacy Officer 
5 | Who counts as an authorised user for volunteer contact details? | Privacy Officer 

## 5. Reflection
This week I learned to turn words like easy and fast into clear statements with an operation, conditions, a threshold, and a check. I also gave each priority a reason. I noticed that FR05 depends on who verifies qualifications, which is still unresolved, so I marked it provisional. Next, I will confirm the targets and priorities with stakeholders before treating them as agreed.
